// KNOWN, TRACKED, ACCEPTED LIMITATION -- this file lives in 10_known_bugs/,
// which scripts/test_runner.sh deliberately excludes from the main suite, so
// it is not asserted either way by `./scripts/test_runner.sh`.
//
// A function whose ENTIRE body is a single, provably-exhaustive `compare`
// (every enum variant covered, or an explicit `otherwise`), with a `return`
// in every arm and NO trailing statement after the compare, is incorrectly
// rejected with "The function 'describe' promises to give back a result,
// but sometimes it doesn't!" -- even though every actual runtime path
// through this function does return.
//
// This is a deliberate, accepted trade-off, not an oversight: see
// src/CFG.cpp's CFGBuilder::visitCompare() doc comment for the full
// history. Two earlier, more "precise" versions of that function DID
// correctly recognize this case as exhaustive-and-terminating -- but both
// versions, in turn, incorrectly rejected a WIDELY-used, clearly
// intentional idiom throughout this codebase's own test suite: the same
// kind of exhaustive compare, immediately followed by a redundant "belt
// and suspenders" trailing `return` (see
// tests/14_enums/20_compare_param_subject_exhaustive.tr, and pre-existing
// tests like 13_dsa_state_machine.tr, 15_dsa_direction_navigation.tr,
// 16_dsa_priority_levels.tr, and others -- confirmed to break at least 7
// existing tests each time). Since the codebase clearly prefers "always
// allow a trailing statement after compare" over precise unreachable-code
// detection for this one construct, `compare` is now NEVER treated as a
// hard CFG terminator by itself, which means THIS file's specific shape
// (no trailing return at all) is the one case that still gets a false
// positive.
//
// Workaround: add a trailing `return` after the compare block (as every
// other function in this codebase already does) -- see
// 20_compare_param_subject_exhaustive.tr for the identical function with
// that one line added.
//
// Fixing this properly (recognizing compare-without-trailing-code as
// terminating while still allowing compare-with-trailing-code) would need
// CFGBuilder::visitBlock() to look ahead within the SAME block to see
// whether the compare is the block's last statement before deciding how to
// treat its AllTerminate result, rather than visitCompare() deciding in
// isolation. Flagged for a future CFG pass rather than chased down here.

enum Direction { North, East, South, West }

fn describe(d: Direction): int {
    compare d {
        matches Direction::North:
            return 1;
        done
        matches Direction::East:
            return 2;
        done
        matches Direction::South:
            return 3;
        done
        matches Direction::West:
            return 4;
        done
    }
}

fn main(): int {
    assert(describe(Direction::North) == 1, "");
    return 0;
}
